Article 12

The duties of the PUG Focal Point include:

a. assisting policy makers of work units within the scope of duties, main tasks and functions of their agencies to take planned steps fully when there are gender gaps;
b. encouraging and assisting work units to evaluate and improve mandates, policies, programs, projects, activities and budgets to be more gender responsive;
c. facilitating the implementation of gender sensitivity training, gender analysis training and developing gender work networks with agencies or institutions or organizations and other work units, both government and non-government;
d. seeking the implementation of gender analysis as one of the stages in every development process starting from the planning, implementation, monitoring and evaluation stages;
e. elaborating and following up on policies and development implementation programs to be gender responsive that are implicit in the PD Strategic Plan, PD Work Plan and RKPD;
f. participating in various activities organized by the PUG Working Group and/or national PUG working group; and
g. preparing activity reports periodically and reporting them to the regent through the chairperson of the PUG working group.

Article 7

The duties of the PUG Working Group include:

a. promoting and facilitating dialogue between work units within the scope of regional government;
b. developing work networks in accordance with the main duties, functions and authorities given by the leadership in efforts to realize gender equality and justice;
c. assigning the technical team to prepare the PUG RAD in order to realize gender equality and justice adjusted to the RPJMD periodization;
d. elaborating and following up on policies and implementation programs to be gender responsive that are implicit and explicit in the RPJMD;
e. coordinating the establishment of PUG Focal Points in each PD or work unit;
f. promoting PUG in each PD;
g. strengthening the capacity of PUG Focal Points in preparing gender-responsive budgets by the technical team;
h. overseeing the preparation of gender-responsive planning and budgeting in each PD;
i. including gender indicators in the guidelines for preparing the APBD;
j. ensuring that gender-responsive budgets become a consideration in the discussion and determination of the APBD together with the DPRD;
k. holding coordination meetings every 6 (six) months to ensure the implementation of PUG in the Region;
l. preparing and submitting reports on PUG implementation to the regent every 6 (six) months; and
m. holding coordination meetings before the preparation of the RKPD.

